@@ -73,6 +73,23 @@ The default minimum reliable group size is 30 and can be configured from 2 to 50
 
 This rule prevents tiny cells from dominating the score; it does not make larger cells automatically representative.
 
+## Assessability guards
+
+A fairness risk score is only meaningful when the data actually supports a
+between-group comparison. ChaosHire therefore marks an audit **not assessable**
+(grade `N/A`, score 0, with explicit reasons) when:
+
+- the dataset is empty;
+- every candidate received the same decision (all selected or all rejected), so selection rates carry no information;
+- no protected attribute was supplied or detected; or
+- no protected attribute has at least two groups above the minimum reliable size.
+
+Without these guards a model that rejects everybody would score a perfect
+100/A, because ratios of identical rates are trivially equal. Not-assessable
+audits still report their group metrics for inspection, appear as `N/A` in the
+dashboard, reports, and audit history, and raise a HIGH finding in the review
+agent.
+
 ## Intersectional analysis
 
 ChaosHire creates every pairwise combination of selected protected attributes. For example:
@@ -96,3 +113,33 @@ The current risk score uses primary, non-intersectional attributes only:
 - Transparency features: 15 points
 
 The score is a transparent ChaosHire product indicator—not an official certification from a regulator, standards body, or government agency.
+
+## The trained reference model (TalentFit v3)
+
+`trained` is not hand-written: it is a scikit-learn `LogisticRegression`
+(C=1.0, lbfgs, max_iter=1000) fitted to the same deterministic 1,000-candidate
+fixture, predicting the `qualified` label from the normalized merit features
+(skills, experience, education, certifications) plus two "neutral" résumé
+signals a real training team would likely keep: college prestige and career
+gap. Gender, ethnicity, and age features are withheld from training and
+recorded as explicit zero weights.
+
+The fitted decision function is affinely calibrated onto the platform's [0,1]
+score convention so the logistic probability-0.5 boundary lands exactly on the
+shared decision threshold (0.5) and the observed score extremes fit inside the
+range. Coefficients are rounded to six decimals and pinned to
+`chaoshire/artifacts/trained_model.json` with a SHA-256 digest; runtime code
+never trains and never imports scikit-learn, so the published numbers cannot
+drift with library upgrades. `python -m chaoshire train --check` re-fits the
+model and fails when fresh training disagrees with the pinned artifact on more
+than 10% of demo decisions.
+
+What the audit finds: certificate **71/C** and perfect chaos resilience
+(**100/100** — zero flips in all five experiments, because the model is
+genuinely blind to protected attributes), yet gender disparate impact is
+**0.78** and age-band impact **0.73**, both below the four-fifths rule.
+Proxy signals and label noise reproduce the disparity without the model ever
+seeing a protected attribute: blind training does not guarantee blind
+decisions. That contrast — perfect counterfactual resilience alongside a
+failing disparate-impact ratio — is exactly the kind of finding a single
+evaluation lens would miss, and it is why ChaosHire reports both.
